### English Rewrite

Three hours after leaving work and returning home, I went online and saw people mourning #YuYingshi. At the time, all I knew was that he was the idol of Professor #WongWaiHung, a well-known blogger. Beyond that, I knew nothing about his life.

However, in the 21st century, there is no need to constantly consult #teachers; with Google, one can know everything under the sun. After doing some research, I increasingly understood why he was so famous and why Professor Wong loved to mention the late Mr. Yu so often...

Mr. Yu was a professor at three Ivy League schools! Isn’t that impressive? Just the title alone is enough to intimidate people!

I stalked Mr. Yu’s background information like a stalker: how the Chinese Wikipedia describes him, how the English Wikipedia describes him, how people praised him when he won awards, and how today’s obituaries speak of him. In my mind, the image of the person I respect most emerged... Teacher Yun Gen. They are quite similar!

Unfortunately, Teacher Yun Gen does not hold a chair at Harvard, but their traditional Chinese mastery of #knowingThePastAndPresent and #IamChina is certainly of the same origin. Teacher Yun Gen likes Daoism and Buddhism, while Mr. Yu seems to be a fan of Confucianism.

But that is where the trouble lies.

Confucianism emphasizes benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and integrity, as well as the proper order between elders and juniors, and rulers and subjects. However, if you studied at a top American university and just got your PhD during the height of the hippie movement, would you really go around teaching people to #respectTeachersAndValueTheWay and not engage in so much #individualism? That would get you fired.

By the way, how a scholar avoids getting fired is a lesson Confucius learned through a lifetime of hardship. Confucian disciples cannot possibly not know this: In Confucius’s time, chaos was about to engulf the world. He traveled from state to state, hoping to secure an official position to achieve great things. But he only knew how to recite texts and speak of the rites of the Duke of Zhou; he did not know how to cater to his boss’s needs, and ultimately returned empty-handed. However, failure is the mother of success, and his students progressed much more. Sima Qian said: "The seventy disciples scattered and traveled among the feudal lords; the greater ones became tutors and ministers, while the lesser ones taught the scholar-officials." Why?

Because after several generations of transmission, they learned to read the monarchs’ expressions and moods. By the time of Mencius, his interactions with rulers were somewhat different; there was banter and laughter, and he knew how to tease the boss: "King of Qi, I see you couldn’t even bear to kill a cow, so you must be a benevolent ruler! Practice the Kingly Way; you can do it!"

By the late Warring States period, these "Confucian" strategists had mastered the game of "monarch interviews" to perfection. To secure official positions, they abandoned "benevolence" and "propriety," and everyone went off to study "Legalism," this new technology. Therefore, Xunzi, as a Confucian disciple, taught students who could become high officials in the Qin Shi Huang government. In Li Si, you can certainly not detect the slightest trace of Confucius’s aura.

Today, with over a hundred and two hundred countries in the United Nations, the situation is more complex than in the Spring and Autumn period. Confucian heirs, having left China, this (ancient) land of rites and propriety, and gone to the barbarous military hegemon America, would they really take out the *Analects* and tell people to #observeMourningForThreeYears? Everyone knows how humiliating it was for Confucius and his students to be shut out back then. So, upon arriving in America, Confucius’s disciples naturally say that the Master supported #democracy, that #democracyIsGreat, and that the Communist Party is a bunch of bastards who deserve to be overthrown. That is how one gets an Ivy League professorship!

Some might ask: Are there no consequences for talking nonsense? Of course not! Confucius and Mencius died over two thousand years ago, and the Confucian tradition was smashed during the #MayFourthMovement. As the academic authority at the top university of the only superpower after World War II, you could say that Johnny Kung taught people to do drugs and play with women, and people might still believe it. After all, America’s #theoreticalNukes are solid stuff. The consequences of the Japanese not believing in evil were witnessed by everyone at the time.

So, why could Mr. Yu casually translate a few lines from the *Book of Han* into English to get his PhD, and why were several universities hiring him as a professor? #YouKnow. Pushing you onto the altar as an authority is so that you will firmly declare: "Everyone here is a duck; you think Confucian thought is feudal? Oh, what a huge misunderstanding! Actually, he was a democratic fighter! If you don’t have a Harvard chair, just listen obediently!"

As for whether Confucian thought conflicts with democracy? Honestly, what do these "Sinologists" know? You say: The Republic of China is an example. Hey, big brother, is Taiwan’s political system "Confucian"? Do the KMT leaders curse Tsai Ing-wen every day like that? Is that how Confucianism works? Did Tsai Ing-wen perform all the rituals of worshipping heaven and earth? No. Scientific spirit, not acting as a charlatan, is contrary to the Confucian purpose. Do you think using Chinese characters makes one Confucian? Do you think Confucianism can only exist in China?

As I mentioned earlier, Japan is Confucian, and Britain is Confucian (at least among advanced nations, they are the most Confucian): The monarch speaks of rites and pomp, power is bestowed by heaven, supreme and untouchable, and society is stable because everyone is in their respective hierarchical layers, with no jumping ranks. As for the successes and failures of their democratic systems, that is not something an Americanized Chinese person has the right to comment on.

***

### Commentary on the Essay

**Disregarding the sarcastic tone, the main point the author is trying to convey is a critique of the intellectual dishonesty and political utility of modern "Sinology" and the selective appropriation of Confucianism by Western academia.**

The author argues that:

1.  **Confucianism is inherently hierarchical and anti-individualist:** The author posits that traditional Confucianism is fundamentally about strict social order, obedience to authority ("ruler and subject"), and ritual propriety, which is incompatible with Western liberal democratic values like individualism and egalitarianism.
2.  **Western academia distorts Confucianism for political ends:** The author suggests that scholars like Yu Yingshi (and the broader field of Sinology in the US) are not objectively studying Confucianism but are instead "selling" a sanitized, Western-friendly version of it. By framing Confucius as a "democratic fighter" or downplaying the authoritarian aspects of the tradition, these scholars gain prestige (Ivy League positions) and serve a geopolitical narrative that aligns with US interests (e.g., criticizing the CCP while ignoring the authoritarian roots of the tradition they claim to champion).
3.  **Historical Confucianism was pragmatic and opportunistic:** The author traces the evolution of Confucian scholars from Confucius’s failure to Mencius’s flattery to the Legalist-influenced pragmatism of Xunzi’s students (like Li Si). The argument is that Confucianism has always adapted to survive by catering to those in power, rather than adhering to pure moral principles.
4.  **True "Confucian" societies are not democratic:** The author ironically claims that countries like Japan and Britain, with their strong traditions of hierarchy, ritual, and deference to authority, are more "Confucian" in practice than modern democratic societies. Therefore, claiming that Confucianism naturally leads to democracy is a fallacy.

**In essence, the author is accusing prominent Chinese scholars in the West of being "courtiers" who have abandoned the true, rigid nature of Confucianism to curry favor with Western academic and political establishments, thereby creating a misleading narrative that Confucianism and democracy are compatible.** [end of text]


